    {
      "id": "gcf-fc05",
      "front": "GCF of the variables",
      "back": "Use the <b>lowest</b> exponent of each variable that is in every term. x⁵ and x³ → x³.<br>If a variable is missing from any term, it is not part of the GCF."
    },
    {
      "id": "gcf-fc06",
      "front": "Factoring out the GCF: steps",
      "back": "1. Find the GCF (numbers, then each variable).<br>2. Divide every term by the GCF.<br>3. Write GCF(what's left).<br>4. Check by distributing.<br>6x² + 9x = 3x(2x + 3)"
    },
    {
      "id": "gcf-fc07",
      "front": "Factoring vs. distributing",
      "back": "They undo each other. Distributing: 3x(2x + 3) → 6x² + 9x. Factoring: 6x² + 9x → 3x(2x + 3)."
    },
    {
      "id": "gcf-fc08",
      "front": "Check by distributing",
      "back": "Multiply the outside by <b>every</b> term inside. If you get the original back, it's right.<br>4(x + 1) = 4x + 4 ✓"
    },
    {
      "id": "gcf-fc09",
      "front": "Mistake: forgetting the 1",
      "back": "When a whole term is the GCF, a <b>1</b> stays behind.<br>4x + 4 = 4(x + 1), not 4(x) or 4x.<br>9x² + 3x = 3x(3x + 1)."
    },
    {
      "id": "gcf-fc10",
      "front": "Mistake: not taking the full GCF",
      "back": "12x² + 8x = 2x(6x + 4) is equal, but 6x + 4 still has a 2 in common.<br>Factored completely: <b>4x(3x + 2)</b>. If the inside still shares a factor, keep going."
    },
    {
      "id": "gcf-fc11",
      "front": "Factoring out a negative",
      "back": "If the first term is negative, you can take out −GCF. Every sign inside flips.<br>−8x² − 12x = −4x(2x + 3)."
    },
    {
      "id": "gcf-fc12",
      "front": "How many terms inside?",
      "back": "The parentheses have the same number of terms as the original. 12y⁴ + 18y³ − 6y² (3 terms) = 6y²(2y² + 3y − 1) (3 terms)."
    }
